Give every callback URL a secret of its own.

The relay patch 0006 adds hands an extension sign-in back to the app through
`vscodroid://callback`, whose filter is exported and BROWSABLE, and the request
id it names is a counter the workbench restarts at one with every page. What
told a real callback from a forged one was a per-run secret `server.js` wrote
into `callback.html`, and that page is served by `/callback`, which patch 0012
answers before the connection token check. Measured on an API 33 emulator: a
plain `GET /callback` from another uid over loopback returns the page with the
secret in it, so any installed app could read it and then fire the filter with
an OAuth result of its own for a sign-in the user had in flight.

So the secret is minted here instead, one per request, in the callback URL the
extension receives from `asExternalUri` and uses as its redirect. It leaves the
device with the provider and comes back in the query the browser is redirected
to, which `callback.html` already hands to the app inside `uri`. Nothing serves
it: the app compares what arrives with what it read out of the address it was
asked to open, and `/callback` fetched by anything else carries no secret at
all.

What this does not cover, and cannot: the provider sees the redirect URL, so a
provider that keeps it could answer for that one request. That party is the one
the user is signing in to and the one the real callback comes from, and the
value is spent when the sign-in completes.

`crypto.getRandomValues` rather than `Math.random`, because this is a secret and
the page already requires a secure context.

